@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant — the same Checkout/Pay pair shown with «include» (mandatory) vs «extend» (optional), with a note on the semantic difference.
'   usecase:      Contrast «include» (base always calls target) with «extend» (optional, condition-gated extension).
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       E-Commerce
' ============================================================================
title Example Use Case Diagram Variant - Include vs Extend

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' LEFT frame:  «include».  Checkout MANDATORILY invokes Pay.  Pay is part of
'              the Checkout flow; it always happens.  Arrow base .> target.
' RIGHT frame: «extend».   Apply Discount OPTIONALLY extends Checkout only
'              when a discount code is entered.  Arrow extension .> base.
' The two relationships look similar (dashed arrows) but mean OPPOSITES:
'   include => base NEEDS target   (base controls, target mandatory)
'   extend => target ADDS to base  (extension controls, base unaware)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

left to right direction

actor "Customer" as custA
actor "Customer " as custB

' ===== INCLUDE variant ==================================================
package "«include» variant" as inc {
  usecase "Checkout" as incCheckout
  usecase "Pay"      as incPay

  custA --> incCheckout
  incCheckout .> incPay : <<include>>
}

' ===== EXTEND variant ===================================================
package "«extend» variant" as ext {
  usecase "Checkout"      as extCheckout
  usecase "Apply Discount" as extDiscount

  custB --> extCheckout
  extDiscount .> extCheckout : <<extend>>
}

note top of incPay
  <b>«include» — MANDATORY</b>
  Base: Checkout
  Target: Pay
  Checkout ALWAYS invokes Pay.
  Use to factor out shared, required
  behaviour.  The BASE owns the flow.
end note

note top of extDiscount
  <b>«extend» — OPTIONAL</b>
  Base: Checkout
  Extension: Apply Discount
  Condition: { code entered }.
  The base is COMPLETE without it; the
  extension ADDS behaviour only when its
  condition is true.  The EXTENSION
  owns the trigger.
end note

note bottom of extCheckout
  <b>Semantic difference</b>
  «include»: the base use case REQUIRES the target — arrow leaves the base.
  «extend»:  the target use case ENRICHES the base — arrow leaves the target.
  Same glyph family (dashed), opposite direction & intent.  Mixing them up
  is the single most common use-case modelling error.
end note

@enduml
